From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stephen Bates <sbates@raithlin.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 22:33:59 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] ionic: send an unreadable skb with too many frags as a TSO

A Tx descriptor takes 16 buffers: the one it points to and 15 SG
elements. A packet in more pieces than that is linearized, which can't
be done for an unreadable (net_iov) skb, so the driver drops it and
counts it in tx_unreadable_drop.

For a devmem TCP sender that drop is not a lost packet, it is the end
of the connection. TCP retransmits the same skb, tcp_can_collapse()
leaves unreadable skbs alone, so every retransmission has the same
frags and is dropped too, until the socket times out. And the sender
doesn't have to do anything strange to get there. A devmem frag is one
piece of the iovec, so it takes iovec pieces below about a 15th of the
MSS, or pieces of any size that tcp_sendmsg_locked() appends to the
skb at the tail of the write queue: sends of 15 pieces of 64 bytes
with TCP_NODELAY, not waited for, become skbs of 17 frags.

Nothing in the stack limits the frags of a packet to what the device
takes. But the driver can make more packets of it: a TSO descriptor is
one TCP segment with an SG list of its own, and the device writes each
segment's lengths, sequence number, flags and checksums. So send such
an skb with the TSO opcode and an mss of the driver's choosing, small
enough for every descriptor to fit. To the device this is an ordinary
TSO, every descriptor but the last with exactly mss bytes of payload.
To the peer it is the same bytes in shorter segments. To the sending
TCP it is what a middlebox that resegments does: an skb acked in part
stays in the retransmit queue until the rest is, and a retransmission
of it starts at snd_una (tcp_trim_head()).

The mss is the size of the shortest run of 15 consecutive frags, or the
skb's own when that is smaller. A descriptor that needs a 17th buffer
has 15 whole frags in the middle of its payload and at least a byte
before and after them, so it carries more than that; the first
descriptor, which also points to the headers, has the first 15 frags
and a byte more. For 17 frags of 64 bytes that is an mss of 960 and
two descriptors.

The same is done for an unreadable GSO skb with a segment that needs
too many buffers: it goes out with that mss in place of its gso_size.

The skb itself is not changed. It is a clone, and its shinfo is the one
the copy in the socket's retransmit queue has, so gso_size and gso_segs
are left as they are and the mss is handed to ionic_tx_tso(). That
function prepares the headers in the linear area after skb_cow_head(),
as it does for every GSO skb, and those are the clone's own.

This is only done for what the device is known to do right:

 - TCP over IPv4 or IPv6, with the headers and nothing else in the
   linear area, CHECKSUM_PARTIAL, no tunnel, and no IPv6 extension
   headers.
 - TSO enabled for that IP version, since a device told to turn TSO off
   may refuse the opcode, and TSO_ECN as well if CWR is set.
 - No SYN, RST or URG: the device repeats the flags on every segment
   except FIN and PSH, which it leaves for the last, and CWR, for the
   first.
 - IPv4 with DF set and no fragment offset. The device numbers the IDs
   of the segments from the header's, and the stack only set one aside
   (or gso_segs of them), so the IDs can repeat. That is harmless for
   packets that are never fragmented, and only for those.
 - Not on the hardware timestamp queue.
 - At most half a ring of descriptors.

Anything else is dropped and counted as before. The new counter,
tx_unreadable_reseg, counts the skbs sent this way; they also count in
tx_tso, and each segment in tx_pkts.

What remains: the drop, and so the dead connection, when TSO is off or
was not granted, and for a layout that needs more descriptors than
half the ring. The mss chosen is safe but not always the largest that
would fit: one frag of 1400 bytes followed by 16 of one byte goes out
as 95 segments of 15 bytes where three segments of 700 would do. No
known sender does that, and finding the best mss means trying them.

Not tested on ionic hardware. The descriptors are those of a TSO with a
small gso_size, but whether a device takes an mss this small (15 at
the least) has only been tried on an emulator.

[ rocm-ernic: backport of a seventh patch for the RFC series "ionic:
  support netmem TX" (based on v7.4-rc3, not posted) to v7.2.6 with
  patches/srq, patches/p4 and patches/devmem-tx 0001-0007. The change to
  Documentation/.../pensando/ionic.rst is left out, since the DKMS
  sources don't include that file; the rest of the diff differs only in
  its line numbers. ]
